// EXPECT-DIAG: E124
// EXPECT-DIAG: Cannot compare struct values with '=='
// EXPECT-DIAG: 'Set<Big>.add' requires 'Set<Big>.contains'
// EXPECT-DIAG: the error itself, inside 'Set<Big>.bucket_find'
// The diagnostic prints the source line of its PRIMARY location as the
// snippet, so these two lines pin the anchor: it is this file's own call (the
// outermost user-code request), and no longer the line inside set.tr -- the
// innermost requester -- that it used to point at.
// EXPECT-DIAG: s.add(Big::new(1, 2, 3));
// EXPECT-NO-DIAG: self.bucket_find(item)
// Regression test: D7 (docs/development/changes/TYPE-002.md, S15) -- the
// plan's own testing scope (ARCHITECTURE_OVERHAUL.md §8's TYPE-002 entry)
// named `fail_generic_requires_operator.tr`, deferred twice (S12a -> S13a/
// S13b) on the grounds it "is not exercisable yet". That reasoning went
// stale once struct-bound generics were monomorphized (S13a/S13b): a `T`
// lacking an operation its generic body needs is now a real, reachable
// instantiation-site error.
//
// `Set<T>` requires `T` to support `==` (`Set.index_of()`'s own
// `self.items[i] == item`) -- a plain struct doesn't, by design (no
// trait/constraint system, F-GEN-3 frozen; struct `==` requires an
// explicit `operator ==` overload, tests/18_operator_overload/
// 02_struct_operator_eq.tr). `s.add(...)` on a `Set<Big>` (no operator==
// overload for Big) must be rejected, not silently miscompiled.
//
// The D25 gap this file used to disclose is closed (LEC-005): the primary
// diagnostic anchors at THIS file's `s.add(...)` call -- the outermost
// user-code request -- not inside `lib/std/collections/set.tr`, and it prints
// the real chain from there (`Set<Big>.add` -> `Set<Big>.contains` -> ...).
// The runner can only match text, so the chain hops and the error code are
// asserted below, and the primary location is pinned through the source
// snippet the diagnostic prints for it (the analysis facade does not run the
// monomorphizer, so tests/api/analysis_test.cpp cannot see this error).
import set::Set;

struct Big { a: int, b: int, c: int }
fn Big.new(x: int, y: int, z: int): void { self.a = x; self.b = y; self.c = z; }

fn main(): int {
    mut s: Set<Big> = Set::default();
    s.add(Big::new(1, 2, 3));
    return 0;
}
